  <h1>How Hostile Work Environment Claims Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>The five elements</h2><p>(1) Plaintiff belongs to a protected class. (2) Harassment was unwelcome. (3) Based on protected characteristic. (4) Severe or pervasive. (5) Employer liability. All five must be established.</p>
  <h2>Severe or pervasive</h2><p>Courts look at: frequency, severity, whether the conduct was physically threatening or humiliating, and whether it unreasonably interfered with work performance. A single extremely severe incident (sexual assault, violent racial attack) can satisfy this element. For less severe conduct, a pattern over time is required.</p>
  <h2>Employer liability</h2><p>For supervisor harassment resulting in tangible employment action: strict liability. For supervisor harassment without tangible action: employer can assert Faragher/Ellerth affirmative defense (reasonable anti-harassment policy + employee unreasonably failed to complain). For co-worker harassment: employer liable only if it knew or should have known and failed to take prompt corrective action.</p>
  <h2>EEOC process</h2><p>File an EEOC charge within 180 days (300 in states with fair employment agencies). EEOC investigates and issues a right-to-sue letter (or makes a cause finding). You then have 90 days to file in federal court. The entire process takes 6 months to 3+ years.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-workplace-harassment">what to do after harassment</a> and <a href="/guides/hostile-work-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
